  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Blueberry Leaf Rust (Naohidemyces vacciniorum) is a fungal pathogen in the Milesinaceae family that infects blueberry plants, causing rust-colored lesions on leaf surfaces. This disease is particularly problematic in the moist climate of New England, where it can reduce plant vigor and berry quality.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Rust-colored pustules on leaf undersides",
      "description": "Look for bright orange to rust-colored, powdery pustules (uredinia) on the abaxial (lower) surface of blueberry leaves",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Yellow spots on upper leaf surface",
      "description": "The adaxial (upper) side of infected leaves typically displays corresponding yellow or pale spots",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Aecial stage on alternate hosts",
      "description": "Cup-shaped structures (aecia) may develop on leaves of alternate hosts in the Ericaceae family",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Naohidemyces vacciniorum thrives in the cool, humid conditions of New England blueberry cultivations, preferring moist environments that favor fungal spore germination and spread.",
